<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!-- Base application theme. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.Light.DarkActionBar">
        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/colorPrimary</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryDark">@color/colorPrimaryDark</item>
        <item name="colorAccent">@color/colorAccent</item>
    </style>

    <!-- postSplashScreenTheme points here, so this is the window the launch
         hands over TO, and its background is what is on screen for any moment
         when neither the system splash nor the WebView is drawing.

         It was the platform default. The parent is DayNight and the app has no
         light theme at all, so on a phone in light mode that default is
         near-white: the seam between the two splash screens, and the moment
         before the first web paint, could both show a pale frame on an app that
         is black everywhere else.

         Painting it with the same colour the two splashes use makes that seam
         unobservable rather than merely brief. It cannot be timed away, because
         how long the WebView takes to paint is not something this app controls.
         The answer is to make the gap the right colour rather than to try to
         make it short.

         windowBackground and not android:statusBarColor: from API 35 the
         platform enforces edge-to-edge and ignores statusBarColor, and
         targetSdkVersion is 36. See #195. -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBar" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:background">@null</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/splashBackground</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar">false</item>
    </style>


    <!-- The launch window, and the only part of startup Android lets an app
         style. From Android 12 the system draws this itself: an icon on a
         colour, with the icon capped at 288dp and any animation capped at
         1000ms. There is no way to put a wordmark here, which is why the
         animated one lives in the WebView (AppSplash.tsx) and this exists only
         to make the seam between them invisible.

         The stock template set android:background to a full-screen
         @drawable/splash PNG. That attribute is not what Theme.SplashScreen
         reads, so on every device running 12 or newer it did nothing at all:
         the app was showing the platform default. The three items below are the
         ones that actually paint.

         postSplashScreenTheme matters as much as the colour: without it the
         window keeps the splash theme after handing over, and the first WebView
         frame draws against the wrong background.

         REPLACING THE ICON. drawable/splash_icon.png is the only file to swap,
         and there are two rules it has to keep, both learned the hard way:

           1. Whatever fills the space around the mark must be exactly the same
              colour as windowSplashScreenBackground, or fully transparent.
              Android masks this drawable to a CIRCLE, so any plate that differs
              from the background renders as a visible disc with its corners cut
              off. The artwork shipped here originally had a dark slate plate on
              a black background and did exactly that.
           2. Keep the mark inside the middle two thirds of the canvas. The
              outer third is what the circular mask eats. -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/splashBackground</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/splash_icon</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/AppTheme.NoActionBar</item>
    </style>
</resources>